- How would you extend to weighted graphs and use Dijkstra with path counting?
- How to adapt for multiple sources and answering many queries efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP, memoization, and bottom-up tabulation on a classic polygon triangulation problem with solution counting. It checks careful state design and combinatorial counting under optimal substructure.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on polygons: dp[i][j] = min over i<k<j of dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j]
- Counting optimal solutions: parallel cnt[i][j] updated when a new min is found or matched
- Circular handling: fix a root vertex and solve a linearized version

## Code Example
```javascript
function minTriangulation(w){
  const n = w.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const cnt = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n-1;i++){ dp[i][i+1] = 0; cnt[i][i+1] = 1; }
  for(let len=2; len<n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len<n; i++){
      const j = i+len;
      for(let k=i+1; k<j; k++){
        const val = dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){ dp[i][j] = val; cnt[i][j] = cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
        else if(val === dp[i][j]){ cnt[i][j] += cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {minCost: dp[0][n-1], ways: cnt[0][n-1]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a recurrence for a simple constraint (no adjacent picks), then implement both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation. Requires correct base cases for N=0 and N=1 and translating to iterative state transitions. Follow-up prompts space optimization and practical coding habits.

## Key Concepts
- DP for non-adjacent max sum (House Robber)
- Top-down memoization with base cases
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Space optimization to O(1)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxNonAdjacent(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const solve = i => {
    if (i < 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = Math.max(nums[i] + solve(i-2), solve(i-1));
    return memo[i];
  };
  return solve(n-1);
}
function maxNonAdjacentTab(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  let prev2 = 0, prev1 = Math.max(0, nums[0]);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++){
    const cur = Math.max(prev1, nums[i] + prev2);
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values or all-negative arrays?
- How would you adapt to a similar constraint with k-adjacent bans (no more than two chosen in any window of size k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to blend BFS intuition with shortest-path techniques under a hop constraint and to reconstruct a path from augmented state.

## Key Concepts
- Augmented state graph: (node, hops)
- Dijkstra with 2D dist and predecessor tracking
- Path reconstruction from (node, hops) chain
- Time/space: O((N*H + M*H) log(N*H))

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathWithHops(graph, S, T, H) {
  // graph: {u: [{to,w}, ...], ...}
  const dist = new Map();
  const prev = new Map();
  // min-heap assumed available: push({time, node, hops})
  const heap = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.time-b.time);
  dist.set(`${S}|0`, 0);
  heap.push({time:0, node:S, hops:0});
  while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    const {time,u,k} = heap.pop();
    if (time !== dist.get(`${u}|${k}`)) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of (graph[u] || [])) {
      const nk = k + 1;
      if (nk > H) continue;
      const nd = time + w;
      const key = `${v}|${nk}`;
      if (nd < (dist.get(key) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(key, nd);
        prev.set(key, `${u}|${k}`);
        heap.push({time: nd, node: v, hops: nk});
      }
    }
  }
  let bestTime = Infinity; let bestKey = null;
  for (let k = 0; k <= H; k++) {
    const key = `${T}|${k}`;
    const d = dist.get(key);
    if (d != null && d < bestTime) { bestTime = d; bestKey = key; }
  }
  const path = [];
  if (bestKey) {
    let cur = bestKey;
    while (cur) {
      const [node] = cur.split('|');
      path.push(node);
      cur = prev.get(cur);
    }
    path.reverse();
  }
  return { time: bestTime, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple equal shortest paths?
- How would you optimize memory for very large graphs?
- How would you adapt if hops constraint is on edges vs. nodes?

